    isbefore: []  # e9bd
    duplicate_of: []  # e9bd
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e9bd
    pr_ref:  # e9bd
    description: "Umbrella for follow-on work after the WI-tihup + WI-mafik bundle (PRs #3674, #3677). PR1 + PR2 shipped the primitives (`ExternalRef`, `ImportScope`, `make_unresolved_edge(dst_ref=…)`, dst_ref-prefer consumer paths) and migrated 8 analyzers — Python + 7 non-Python (java, go, elixir, js_ts, cpp, rust, ruby).\n\nRoughly 90 other analyzers in `hypergumbo-lang-mainstream`, `hypergumbo-lang-common`, and `hypergumbo-lang-extended1` still emit legacy `Edge.dst` strings with `dst_ref = None`. No regression vs. pre-PR1; no benefit either. The structural parsing-bug fix in `ir.create_boundary_nodes` only helps edges that carry `dst_ref`.\n\nPhase 2 is **placeholder / needs_human_review** because:\n- The audit instrument (`test_polyglot_call_site_coverage.py`) only covers 8 fixtures — extending it is a design choice (which languages? prioritized how?), not a mechanical task.\n- The bakeoff signal for Phase 1's value (closing the 7 audited gaps) hasn't landed yet — the `awaits_bakeoff_validation` tags on WI-tihup / WI-mafik need a BROAD cycle to clear before Phase 2 prioritization is data-driven instead of speculative.\n- Many long-tail analyzers emit very few external-target call edges (CSS, INI, JSON, gitignore, dockerfile…), so a mechanical sweep should be scoped, not blanket.\n\nChildren (initially also `needs_human_review`):\n- Bakeoff-validate WI-tihup + WI-mafik via BROAD cycle (prerequisite — produces signal data for prioritization).\n- Extend polyglot fixture to 5-10 additional languages.\n- Sweep-style PR for analyzers that already use `make_unresolved_edge` with a clear module hint.\n\nTrigger condition: after the bakeoff-validation child closes, re-evaluate Phase 2 scope. If the bakeoff signal shows specific un-audited languages contributing FP volume in dead-code or io-boundary chains, those become high-priority retrofit candidates. If the bakeoff signal is muted, Phase 2 may be reduced to the mechanical-sweep child only or closed `wont_do`.\n\nOut of scope (call out, don't re-audit each time):\n- Tooling languages with no call-edge concept (gitignore, ini, properties, yaml_ansible, dockerfile, manifest_targets, json_config, xml_config, toml_config, requirements, jvm_deps) — dst_ref is N/A for their primary edge types (imports / contains, not calls).\n- def_use companion modules (py_def_use, rust_def_use, ts_def_use) — these emit reference edges, not external-target calls.\n- Pure SCIP / IR-backed analyzers (rust_scip) — already structured."  # e9bd
    fields: {}  # e9bd
- op: update  # bb49
  at: '2026-05-14T19:41:14Z'  # bb49
  by: agent  # bb49
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bb49
  clock: 2  # bb49
  nonce: bb49  # bb49
  set:  # bb49
    title: "Long-tail analyzer dst_ref retrofit sweep (umbrella)"  # bb49
    description: "The ~90 long-tail analyzers in `hypergumbo-lang-mainstream`, `hypergumbo-lang-common`, and `hypergumbo-lang-extended1` still emit legacy `Edge.dst` strings with `dst_ref = None`. The structural parsing-bug fix in `ir.create_boundary_nodes` only helps edges that carry `dst_ref`, so these analyzers see no benefit from that fix (and no regression vs. pre-bundle).\n\n## Relationship to WI-mafik (disjoint, not parent-child)\n\nThis item was originally framed as \"WI-mafik Phase 2.\" That framing was wrong — the two items have disjoint scopes that happen to share a PR-vehicle cluster, not a phase relationship.\n\nThe earlier PR bundle (#3674, #3677) shipped the dst_ref primitives (`ExternalRef`, `ImportScope`, `make_unresolved_edge(dst_ref=…)`, dst_ref-prefer consumer paths) and migrated 8 analyzers — Python plus the 7 non-Python languages WI-mafik audits (java, go, elixir, js_ts, cpp, rust, ruby). **WI-mafik owns the audit-via-polyglot-fixture validation of those 8 analyzers.** This item owns the retrofit of the OTHER ~90 long-tail analyzers.\n\n## Why `needs_human_review` (scope decisions need data)\n\nMany long-tail analyzers emit very few external-target call edges — CSS, INI, JSON, gitignore, dockerfile, manifest_targets, etc. A blanket sweep would be effort spent on analyzers whose dst_ref adoption changes nothing observable downstream. Two data sources should drive scoping:\n\n1. **WI-mafik's bakeoff signal on the 8-analyzer migration.** If specific un-audited languages contribute meaningful FP volume in dead-code or io-boundary chains, those become high-priority retrofit candidates. If the bakeoff signal is muted across the board, this item may reduce to a mechanical-sweep child only or close `wont_do`.\n\n2. **Per-analyzer external-target-edge volume on the prospector corpus.** Some long-tail languages (lua, perl, php, scala, groovy) likely emit non-trivial external-target call edges and warrant retrofit; others almost certainly don't.\n\nTrigger condition: after WI-mafik closes (audit + per-language fixes shipped + bakeoff-validated), re-evaluate this item's scope against the bakeoff signal. Convert from `needs_human_review` to `todo_soft` with a prioritized analyzer list, OR close `wont_do` if signal is muted.\n\n## Out of scope (call out, don't re-audit each time)\n\n- Tooling languages with no call-edge concept (gitignore, ini, properties, yaml_ansible, dockerfile, manifest_targets, json_config, xml_config, toml_config, requirements, jvm_deps) — dst_ref is N/A for their primary edge types (imports / contains, not calls).\n- def_use companion modules (py_def_use, rust_def_use, ts_def_use) — these emit reference edges, not external-target calls.\n- Pure SCIP / IR-backed analyzers (rust_scip) — already structured.\n- Validation of the 8 already-migrated analyzers — that's WI-mafik."  # bb49
  remove:  # bb49
    tags: [phase_2]  # bb49
